  "question": {
    "text": "In the given figure the area of the frictionless piston is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> and it is being held in equilibrium by an external force. Now the external force is slowly removed and heat is supplied to the gas due to which the piston displaces by <math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. The natural length of the ideal springs is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. Spring constant is given as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The pressure of the gas in the final situation is<br />\r<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0026_576e78471013.jpg\" />"
  },
